    "api_error_status": null,
    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` and wrote 16 tests in `tests/test_solution.py`. They all pass with `python -m pytest -q tests`. Each client has its own queue of (time, cost) entries. An entry stops counting once it is exactly `window_seconds` old. `retry_after` works out how many of the oldest entries must expire before the requested cost fits. It returns the time until the last of those expires. The tests cover constructor and cost validation, expiry at the exact window boundary, rejected requests not using budget, client independence, `retry_after` and the rule that time can't go backwards. Two of my first-draft tests had wrong expected values, which I corrected. The implementation was right in both cases.\n\nA call with an invalid `cost` raises before the time check, so it doesn't move the \"latest time seen\". The spec doesn't say which should win, so that was my choice.",
